    If you ask whether a better adjacent route exists, one does for many commerce students. A BCom student who likes numbers but fears a clerical start can aim for analytics in an industry they know. Domain depth plus a tool skill is harder to replace than either alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost">Fees, debt and the pay math</h2>

  <p>
    Read every figure above beside the price of reaching it.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Worked example.</strong> Take a total education budget of Rs 10 lakh. The 10% line for college is Rs 1 lakh, and the 20% caution line is Rs 2 lakh. ICAI's three registration fees (Rs 49,000) are 4.9% of that budget. The remaining money pays for living costs, a laptop, software, current learning, coaching if you choose it, repeat attempts and the low-pay years above.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    College is a small part of career success. Keep about 10% of your family's total education budget for the degree, and ask for course-specific evidence above about 20%. Those are strict planning rules, not laws.
  </p>

  <p>
    Avoid education debt unless a credential is legally required. Signing a statutory audit is reserved for chartered accountants, so CA is a case where the credential is the product. For most other commerce routes, the skill and the proof are the product.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">If a foreign master's or a global credential is in the plan</p>
    <p>
      A big loan for a foreign master's is a high-risk bet, since repayment can hinge on a visa, a job abroad and the rupee rate. Think about it only if the course is truly among the best for finance or accounting and the benefit is checked, using top five as a harsh filter and never as a guarantee.
    </p>
    <p>
      Set it against a low-debt route at home. A scholarship you only hope for is not funding, and renewal terms matter. Before you compare pay across countries, take off tax, rent and loan instalments. Entry rules for ACCA, CFA and US CPA are in our guide to professional courses for commerce students.
    </p>
  </div>

